\section{Select a Form Before Filling Its Fields}

Encoding an instruction means selecting a legal machine form and then writing the fields for that form. A mnemonic alone does not determine the size or meaning of a symbolic operand. In Chapter~2, John R. Levine contrasts 390 relative branches, whose signed displacement is scaled by two and added to the branch instruction's address, with SPARC branches, whose displacement is scaled by four and whose short format can split the field across the instruction word \cite[chapter~2]{levine2000linkers}. These are historical architectures described by Levine, not rules about all branch encodings. An encoder must know the architectural base, scale, signedness, and bit locations from the particular form. On a scaled form, alignment is tested before dividing; silently discarding low bits can branch somewhere else.

Consider the Intel manual's long-mode \texttt{JRCXZ}. It branches when \texttt{RCX} is zero, without consulting the status flags. Its form is \texttt{E3} followed by one signed displacement byte, measured from the next instruction. There is no direct \texttt{JRCXZ} with a wider immediate. This makes it useful for studying a deferred field without conflating that problem with selection between a short and longer form. Given a label in the current input section, a forward reference still may not have a known offset when the assembler first sees the instruction.

\section{The Deferred Operation Belongs to the Assembler}

Call an internal instruction to fill such a field a \emph{fixup}. It can identify the containing fragment and offset within the fragment, the symbolic expression, and a field kind with the relevant encoding rules. A source location lets a later diagnostic point at the label operand, not the last line of the file. A fragment-relative location, instead of a provisional absolute offset, remains meaningful if earlier data or padding changes size. At final layout the assembler computes a particular field value or, where appropriate, asks the object writer to represent a dependency it cannot settle.

An ELF relocation is not a copy of that in-memory record. It is a serialized ABI operation with a relocation kind, place, symbol index, and addend supplied as required by its format. It has neither the assembler's arbitrary expression tree nor its source-span semantics. An internal fixup can disappear into the resulting bytes, cause a diagnostic, or give rise to an output relocation if the remaining computation is both permitted and expressible. Levine's Chapter~1 example of a linker adjusting embedded x86 addresses illustrates the later operation. Do not infer from that example that every local branch needs a link-time patch.

