\section{Resolve a Fixed-Width Forward Branch}

Save this fixture as \texttt{skip.s}. It is an encoding test, to be assembled and inspected rather than run.

\begin{verbatim}
.text
    # E3 is fixed; the following byte depends on the label.
    jrcxz .Lempty
    # These five bytes lie between the branch base and target.
    .fill 5, 1, 0x90
.Lempty:
    ret
\end{verbatim}

On x86-64, run \verb|as --64 -o skip.o skip.s|, then inspect with \verb|objdump -dr skip.o| and \verb|readelf -r skip.o|. The opcode at offset zero is \texttt{E3}, the field at offset one is \texttt{05}, and the return instruction \texttt{C3} is at offset seven. Use the next instruction as the base: $7-(0+2)=5$. The field offset is neither the instruction start nor that base. The bytes themselves give a round-trip test: interpret \texttt{05} as signed, add two, and recover seven. The object needs no relocation because the same section base would be added to both terms and cancels.

\Cref{fig:visual-281} distinguishes the field the assembler writes from the base the processor uses.

