One implementation of the field handler uses the final offsets to compute $d=\text{target}-(\text{start}+2)$ in widened integer arithmetic. It checks $-128\le d\le127$ before writing $d\bmod256$ into the reserved byte. The key invariant is that decoding the byte by the selected instruction's rule and adding the architectural base gives the target, not merely that a byte was written. This handler assumes both positions are fixed within the same section and that the reference to the local label cannot be redirected.

A useful unit boundary is between computing the displacement and updating the fragment. The former can mark the result as known, still symbolic, or invalid for this form. The latter should change only the reserved field after its width and bounds agree with the selected encoding. In this case the \texttt{E3} opcode must remain untouched. A zero in the reserved field before resolution is not evidence that the target is the next instruction: only the fixup state says whether that byte is final.

\section{Test Both the Sign and the Failure Path}

Change the fill to 127 bytes and expect \texttt{E3 7F}. Change it to 128 and expect GNU \texttt{as} to diagnose a displacement out of range. Writing \texttt{80} would represent $-128$, not $128$. A backward check puts a local label at offset zero, three one-byte no-operations after it, and the branch at offset three. The base is five and the field is $-5\bmod256=251$, or \texttt{FB}. This verifies sign handling independently of the forward layout. Replacing the instruction with an ordinary conditional branch that examines flags is not a legal way to avoid an overflow diagnostic; it changes the condition being tested. When forms really are interchangeable, however, choosing between them can require revisiting layout, as in Chapter~\ref{ch:ext-283}.
